  "parent": {
    "sections": [
      {
        "heading": "What your child is learning",
        "text": "Two ways to multiply by 8. Because 8 = 4 + 4, an 8s fact is double the 4s fact: 8 × 7 = 4 × 7 + 4 × 7 = 28 + 28 = 56. Because 8 = 5 + 3, it is also the 5s fact plus the 3s fact: 5 × 7 + 3 × 7 = 35 + 21 = 56."
      },
      {
        "heading": "Try it with real things",
        "list": [
          "Make 8 rows of 7 cereal pieces. Cover the bottom 4 rows: 28. Uncover them: 28 + 28 = 56.",
          "Chant a doubling chain: 3, 6, 12, 24 (times 1, 2, 4, 8)."
        ]
      },
      {
        "heading": "Questions to ask",
        "list": [
          "Where is the 8?",
          "What is the 4s fact? What is double that?",
          "Which way is easier for you: 4 + 4 or 5 + 3?"
        ]
      },
      {
        "heading": "Common mistakes",
        "list": [
          "Doubling the 2s fact only once (that gives the 4s fact, not the 8s fact).",
          "Breaking 8 into numbers that do not add up to 8, such as 4 and 3.",
          "Adding the factors: 8 + 6 = 14."
        ]
      },
      {
        "heading": "A note on notation",
        "text": "Some books write the facts without ( ) and some with ( ). Both mean the same thing. Study Scoop uses original practice questions; it never copies the textbook."
      }
    ]
  }
